 \noindent\textbf{Completeness and its limits.} Only Stage~1a is exhaustive, and only up to its bound: for the STT, ReCon and invisible-speculation specifications, \toolname{} finds every minimal violating execution graph of up to six instructions by solving until UNSAT. SPT's Stage~1a hits a cap of 200{,}000 graphs in its four-instruction partition, so SPT's suite is not exhaustive even at that bound, and no run shows that no leakage backbone needs seven or more instructions. Stage~1b draws a capped number of graphs per backbone in a random solver order, and Stage~2 draws instruction choices per test and emits both branch directions, and the executor draws the timing. Stage~2's chain interleaving (pass~4), which can feed a transmitter from a second, independent speculative source (\S\ref{sec:conc-interleave}), is off in our evaluation. To avoid collisions chains write only free registers, and a load transmitter's address is always a register the graph fixes.
