-\noindent\textbf{Coverage limits.} The vocabulary leaves out instruction and speculation types that would be useful to add: indirect jumps, calls, and returns, whose targets come from the BTB and return-stack predictors; atomic read-modify-writes such as \texttt{xchg} and \texttt{lock}-prefixed instructions; faulting instructions as sources of speculation, which the Futuristic model needs; and a second core, for interactions such as an InvisiSpec request that downgrades another core's copy of a line. 
